{"id": "7c1de864-079b-4026-b0bc-18f394934f37", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:08:51.001265", "source": "user", "parent_id": "fa4f9a69-7b09-4aaa-8d7b-0b40191bc761", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Enfoque aprobado en términos generales. Autorizo el trabajo principal (diseño completo en stage=result) con estas condiciones y aclaraciones:\\n\\n1) Sobre tus preguntas Q1–Q6: no dispongo de los detalles del paper fuente (no hay topics ni documentos adicionales en este proyecto), y no voy a inventarlos. Trátalos como supuestos documentados y explícitos en el diseño final, tal como los listaste. El brief confirma solo: comparar el criterio MinED vs EM en BBH; tarea de diseño, sin ejecutar nada.\\n\\n2) Aclaración conceptual: tu distinción entre 'criterio de alineación interno' vs 'evaluador final fijo' es la correcta y la acepto como columna vertebral del diseño. De hecho, es la única forma de que la pregunta tenga sentido sin sesgo circular. Haz explícito y defiende con detalle el control anti-circularidad: el grader final debe ser idéntico e independiente del criterio bajo comparación. Si asumieras que el grader final del paper es EM estricto (lo cual es posible y plausible para BBH), señala ese riesgo de forma prominente como limitación estructural y describe cómo tu E2 (criterio como juez contra oro independiente) lo mitiga.\\n\\n3) Mantén E1 como estudio primario con contraste pareado por tarea y el control de negatividad en el estrato de opción múltiple; eso es lo mejor del plan. Para E2, deja claro que con un pool de salidas sintéticas/pre-proporcionadas solo valida el mecanismo, no el rendimiento real en BBH; no debe presentarse como evidencia empírica del benchmark.\\n\\n4) En el diseño final incluye obligatoriamente: racional de cada decisión, lista completa de controles (grader fijo, semillas, decodificación, umbral τ=1.0 como ancla de equivalencia MinED≈EM, control de negatividad MC), plan de análisis (diferencia pareada por tarea, bootstrap/Wilcoxon, curva de τ, análisis de discrepancias) y sección de factibilidad que distinga claramente qué es ejecutable en este entorno (solo mocks stdlib) vs. lo que requeriría GPU/red, sin afirmar jamás que algo se ejecutó.\\n\\n5) Formato: el diseño debe ser autocontenido y legible sin esta conversación, con una tabla de supuestos que marque cada uno como 'asumido' o 'confirmado por el brief'.\\n\\nEntrega el diseño completo.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "c6b7dffb-960a-40aa-bfa4-ffe4c2e4e487", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:10:26.094536", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "7c1de864-079b-4026-b0bc-18f394934f37", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved. I need to deliver a full, self-contained experimental design at stage=result. Before that, I could run a couple of quick feasibility checks in stdlib (e.g., implement Levenshtein in pure Python and EM, run on synthetic mock data) — but note the instruction: \"No executed experiment or invented outcome is required for this design-only task.\" A small mock smoke test of the metric implementation is a feasibility check, not an experiment. It would strengthen the feasibility section. Let me do a quick stdlib check: implement Levenshtein DP and normalized MinED, verify τ=1.0 equivalence with EM on synthetic strings, and check that difflib isn't needed. This is a design-supporting feasibility probe, not an experimental outcome. I must be careful to present it as an implementation feasibility probe with synthetic strings, not BBH evidence.\n\nLet me write a small script in /tmp (workspace is 100% full — 5.7G free though; use /tmp to be safe).\n\nThen deliver the design.\n\nDesign structure:\n1. Objective and question addressed\n2. Conceptual backbone: alignment criterion vs fixed final grader; anti-circularity control\n3. Overview of studies: E1 (primary), E2 (secondary mechanism check)\n4. Fixed variables / varied variables\n5. Task stratification MC vs free-form; negative control\n6. Metrics and analysis plan: paired differences per task, bootstrap CIs, Wilcoxon signed-rank (note: with 27 tasks, Wilcoxon n=27, two-sided α=0.05 critical value; exact test possible with permutation since scipy absent — but analysis in full env would use scipy; in this env permutation test in stdlib), τ curve, discrepancy analysis\n7. Full controls list\n8. Hypotheses and decision criteria (what counts as \"outperforms\")\n9. Assumption table (asumido/confirmado por el brief)\n10. Feasibility: executable here (stdlib mocks: MinED DP, EM, bootstrap, Wilcoxon-by-permutation, strata bookkeeping) vs requires GPU/network (model inference, BBH download)\n11. Limitations: structural circularity risk if paper's final grader is EM; E2 only mechanism validation, not empirical BBH evidence\n12. Sample-size / compute budget sketch\n13. Reporting checklist / how to interpret outcomes\n\nAlso include: pre-registration style: define primary endpoint (mean BBH accuracy difference MinED−EM under fixed grader), secondary endpoints. Multiple-comparison handling: single primary endpoint avoids multiplicity; stratified results secondary.\n\nAlso, E1 design detail: where does the alignment criterion act? Assumption: within self-consistency answer aggregation / data filtering. Need to define concretely. Since paper details unknown, I define a minimal parameterized pipeline where the criterion has a single well-defined insertion point, and require the same candidate generation for both arms.\n\nConcrete instantiation (documented assumption): For each BBH instance, generate k=10 sampled CoT rollouts at T=0.7 (for consistency arms), plus the greedy answer. Cluster answers by the alignment criterion: EM arm = exact string match after canonical normalization; MinED arm = normalized edit distance ≤ 1−τ (i.e., similarity ≥ τ) greedy clustering / majority under criterion; output the representative answer of the largest cluster. Final scoring: fixed canonical grader (deterministic answer extraction \"Answer is:\" + task-specific exact match against gold, identical for both arms, applied blind to the criterion). This makes EM-vs-MinED a single-module swap. Alternative insertion point: data selection (filter unlabeled instruction data by agreement between two sources) — mention as alternative and state that the design pattern is identical.\n\nτ sweep: τ ∈ {1.0, 0.95, 0.9, 0.8, 0.7}. τ=1.0 with the same normalization = EM anchor (identical outputs → sanity check that pipeline differences are zero; a strong unit test).\n\nCareful: at τ=1.0 normalized edit distance, MinED matches EM only under identical normalization and tie-breaking. So the anchor test: at τ=1.0, arm outputs must be bit-identical to EM arm. Good.\n\nCircularity concern: in self-consistency, majority vote by EM is the standard baseline and its final evaluation by EM scoring creates bias — because EM-aggregation aligns with EM-scoring (favors surface-form-identical answers), giving EM an unfair advantage. Mitigations: (a) final grader uses task-specific canonical answer matching (BBH official metric, which is exact match on the extracted letter/number but with canonicalization that's independent of raw surface strings), (b) E2: independent gold labels (human/second-model adjudication) for whether predicted answer is semantically equivalent to gold, then compute each criterion's AUROC/accuracy as judge, (c) report sensitivity: re-score E1 with alternative grader variants (case/punctuation-normalized EM, human spot-check of 200 discrepant cases).\n\nAlso mention: the criterion's normalization function must not be given to the grader; keep normalization separate.\n\nAnalysis: per-task accuracy difference d_i for i=1..27 across s=3 seeds (mean over seeds), primary = mean d_i, 95% CI via nonparametric bootstrap over tasks (10k resamples), Wilcoxon signed-rank (exact, n=27) — with note about non-independence across tasks; also mixed model as sensitivity (seed-level). Holm correction for secondary stratified tests.\n\nCompute budget estimate: 27 tasks × ~250–1000 examples... BBH has 250 examples per task (except boolean 660? actually causal_judgment 187, date_understanding 250, ... total ~6511 examples, plus cot prompts 3-shot). k=10 rollouts × 27 tasks × ~241 avg = ~6511 prompts × 10 = ~65k generations + few-shot prompts. Both arms share rollouts → generate once, apply both criteria offline → E1 is essentially free in generation terms! Key efficiency: single shared rollout pool, criteria applied post hoc. That's an important design feature: same rollouts, both criteria evaluated post-hoc → perfect pairing, zero extra inference cost. Great.\n\nToken estimate: ~65k generations × ~300 tokens output = ~20M output tokens. Doable on a single A100/H100 with vLLM in hours for a 7B model. Provide budget table.\n\nFeasibility in this environment: stdlib-only mock validation (I'll actually run a smoke probe to show the metric implementations and anchor test work — clearly labeled as implementation probe on synthetic strings, NOT BBH evidence).\n\nLet me run that probe now.\n\nAlso disk check earlier: /workspace nearly full, so use /tmp.\n\nLet me write /tmp/metric_probe.py.\n\nThen end_researcher_turn with stage=result containing full design in answer. The answer will be long — that's fine.\n\nLet me do the probe.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "mkdir -p /tmp/probe && cat > /tmp/probe/metric_probe.py <<'PY'\n\"\"\"Implementation feasibility probe (stdlib only). NOT an experiment on BBH.\nUses synthetic string pairs to (a) verify a pure-Python Levenshtein,\n(b) verify the tau=1.0 equivalence anchor MinED ~= EM,\n(c) verify bootstrap + permutation paired test run without scipy/numpy.\"\"\"\nimport random, itertools\n\ndef lev(a, b):\n    if len(a) < len(b): a, b = b, a\n    prev = list(range(len(b) + 1))\n    for i, ca in enumerate(a, 1):\n        cur = [i]\n        for j, cb in enumerate(b, 1):\n            cur.append(min(prev[j] + 1, cur[j-1] + 1, prev[j-1] + (ca != cb)))\n        prev = cur\n    return prev[-1]\n\ndef neds(a, b):                      # normalized edit distance in [0,1]\n    d = max(len(a), len(b))\n    return lev(a, b) / d if d else 0.0\n\ndef canon(s):                        # canonicalization used ONLY by the criterion\n    return \" \".join(s.lower().strip().replace(\".\", \"\").replace(\",\", \"\").split())\n\ndef match_em(p, g):                  # EM arm: exact match on canonical text\n    return canon(p) == canon(g)\n\ndef match_mined(p, g, tau):          # MinED arm: neds <= 1 - tau\n    return neds(canon(p), canon(g)) <= 1 - tau\n\ndef cluster_majority(ans, match):    # greedy criterion-based clustering, largest cluster\n    reps = []                         # list of (representative, count, members)\n    for a in ans:\n        for r in reps:\n            if match(a[0], r[0]):\n                r[1] += 1; r[2].append(a[1]); break\n        else:\n            reps.append([a, 1, [a[1]]])\n    reps.sort(key=lambda r: (-r[1], r[0]))   # deterministic tie-break\n    return reps[0][0]\n\n# (a) sanity vs brute-force difflib-free reference on random small strings\nrnd = random.Random(0); bad = 0\nfor _ in range(300):\n    a = \"\".join(rnd.choice(\"abc\") for _ in range(rnd.randint(0, 6)))\n    b = \"\".join(rnd.choice(\"abc\") for _ in range(rnd.randint(0, 6)))\n    if lev(a, b) != lev(b, a) or lev(a, b) > max(len(a), len(b)): bad += 1\nprint(\"lev symmetric/bounded violations:\", bad)\n\n# (b) tau=1.0 anchor: MinED must agree with EM on 5k random answer/gold pairs\nwords = [\"a\", \"b\", \"cat\", \"cats\", \"categorically\", \"42\", \"forty-two\", \"yes\", \"no\", \" true \", \"True.\"]\ndis = 0\nfor _ in range(5000):\n    p, g = rnd.choice(words), rnd.choice(words)\n    if match_em(p, g) != match_mined(p, g, 1.0): dis += 1\nprint(\"anchor tau=1.0 disagreements vs EM:\", dis, \"/\", 5000)\nfor p, g in [(\"cat.\", \"Cat\"), (\"cats\", \"cat\"), (\"forty-two\", \"42\"), (\"yes\", \"no\")]:\n    print(f\"  {p!r} vs {g!r}: EM={match_em(p,g)} MinED0.9={match_mined(p,g,0.9)} neds={neds(canon(p),canon(g)):.3f}\")\n\n# (c) paired analysis primitives, stdlib only\nseeds = 3; rng = random.Random(7)\nd = [round(rng.gauss(0.6, 3.0), 3) for _ in range(27)]          # per-task acc diff (pp), 27 BBH tasks\nboot = [sum(s) / len(s) for s in ([ (rng.choice(d) for _ in range(27)) ] for _ in range(0))]  # placeholder\nbs = []\nfor _ in range(10000):\n    s = [d[i] for i in (rng.randrange(27) for _ in range(27))]\n    bs.append(sum(s) / 27)\nbs.sort(); ci = (bs[int(0.025*len(bs))], bs[int(0.975*len(bs))])\nobs = sum(d) / 27\nsign = sorted(abs(x) for x in d); ranks = {v: i + 1 for i, v in enumerate(sign)}\nWp = sum(ranks[abs(x)] for x in d if x > 0)\nnull = []\nfor _ in range(20000):                                            # exact-ish permutation (sign-flip)\n    null.append(sum(ranks[abs(x)] * (1 if rng.random() < .5 else -1) * (1 if x > 0 else -1) for x in d) / 1)\n    null[-1] = abs(null[-1])\npval = (1 + sum(1 for v in null if v >= abs(2*Wp - sum(ranks.values())))) / (1 + len(null))\nprint(f\"paired mean diff={obs:.2f}pp bootstrap95%CI=({ci[0]:.2f},{ci[1]:.2f}) permutation two-sided p~{pval:.4f}\")\nPY\npython3 /tmp/probe/metric_probe.py", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b082075d527ce4ef", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-b082075d527ce4ef", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"mkdir -p /tmp/probe && cat > /tmp/probe/metric_probe.py <<'PY'\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"Implementation feasibility probe (stdlib only). NOT an experiment on BBH.\\nUses synthetic string pairs to (a) verify a pure-Python Levenshtein,\\n(b) verify the tau=1.0 equivalence anchor MinED ~= EM,\\n(c) verify bootstrap + permutation paired test run without scipy/numpy.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nimport random, itertools\\n\\ndef lev(a, b):\\n    if len(a) < len(b): a, b = b, a\\n    prev = list(range(len(b) + 1))\\n    for i, ca in enumerate(a, 1):\\n        cur = [i]\\n        for j, cb in enumerate(b, 1):\\n            cur.append(min(prev[j] + 1, cur[j-1] + 1, prev[j-1] + (ca != cb)))\\n        prev = cur\\n    return prev[-1]\\n\\ndef neds(a, b):                      # normalized edit distance in [0,1]\\n    d = max(len(a), len(b))\\n    return lev(a, b) / d if d else 0.0\\n\\ndef canon(s):                        # canonicalization used 